rom Release 00.0, which are marked as \"not started.\" This means even though those are the earliest incomplete items, I can't propose tasks for them since they don't have ready requirements. I need to focus on the SRDs that do appear in requirement_states and match them against the earliest releases that need work. So if an SRD isn't listed in requirement_states, that means all its requirements are already complete or skipped—only the SRDs that appear in the list have requirements still in \"ready\" status. Looking at which SRDs have ready requirements and mapping them to their releases, I can see srd004-ts belongs to Release 15.0, while srd079-base32, srd080-base64, and srd081-basenc all map to Release 09.1, and srd082-stat goes to Release 10.0.\n\nContinuing through the rest of the SRDs with ready requirements, I'm seeing srd083-truncate, srd084-link, and srd085-sync all belong to Release 10.0, then srd089-chmod, srd090-chgrp, and srd091-chown map to Release 10.1, followed by srd092-mkfifo, srd093-mknod, srd094-nice, and srd095-nohup in Release 11.0, with srd096-users, srd097-who, and srd098-pinky in Release 11.1, and srd099-shred, srd100-chroot, and srd101-install in Release 12.0, with srd102-tsort still being mapped.\n\nLooking at the next batch, srd102-tsort, srd103-pathchk, and srd104-test are Release 12.1, then srd106-df, srd107-dir, srd108-vdir, and srd109-dircolors are Release 13.0, with srd105-stty, srd110-pr, and srd111-ptx jumping to Release 15.0, and srd112-chronic, srd113-pee, and srd114-vidir in Release 14.0. So the earliest release with all ready requirements is actually Release 09.1 with base32, base64, and basenc, but I need to reconsider what the actual release constraints are.\n\nThe distinction between code_status and requirement_states is important—code_status shows whether code exists in the repo, but requirement_states tracks the pipeline status. Even if code_status says \"not started,\" the requirements might already be complete or skipped in the pipeline. The constraint is to only propose tasks for R-items with \"ready\" status, so I should focus on Release 09.1 as the earliest with ready requirements.\n\nNow I'm checking the specific requirements for srd079-base32.\n\n1, srd080-base64, and srd081-basenc. Base32 has 11 ready requirements, base64 has 11 ready requirements, and basenc has 12 ready requirements—all marked as ready to work on.\n\nI'm realizing I can't actually read the SRDs or requirements.yaml due to the constraint against using tools, so I'll need to assume default weights of 1 for each requirement unless specified otherwise. Looking at the package contracts, srd088-encutil stands out as the encoding utility package that ties together base32, base64, and basenc functionality.\n\nFor the task structure, I need to consider whether cmd/ packages like these need separate contract tasks—they're typically just main.go files without exported types, so the contract requirement might apply more to pkg/ packages. Let me think through what the constraint actually requires here. Since base32 and base64 are structurally identical and both rely on the already-implemented pkg/encutil, I could combine them into a single implementation task, then handle basenc separately. That gives me room for focused test coverage across the utilities while staying within the 4-task limit. I need to account for the weight constraints when splitting these tasks. With a maximum weight of 4 per task and each requirement weighing 1, I can fit at most 4 requirements per task. That means base32 needs 3 tasks, base64 needs 3 tasks, and basenc needs 3 tasks—totaling 9 tasks just for the implementations, which might exceed my overall task budget.\n\nBut I'm limited to 4 tasks total, and I also need to split implementation and tests into separate tasks. Given that Release 09.1 has all three utilities, I should focus on base32 first since it's alphabetically earliest, then figure out how to distribute the remaining requirements